<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dushanbe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stalinabad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dushanbe is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a>, on the Varzob river in the Hisor valley. A market village named after its Monday bazaar, it was chosen as capital of the Tajik ASSR in 1924 and grew rapidly as a planned Soviet city. It was called Stalinabad from 1929 to 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dushanbe.jpg" alt="Dushanbe" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Harangozó · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_with_buildings,_Dushanbe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dushanbe lies in the Hisar Valley, at 750 to 930 metres above sea level, at the confluence of the Varzob and the Kofarnihon rivers. The Hisar range, which reaches up to 4,000 metres, bounds it on the north and east, and the Babatag, Aktau, Rangontau and Karatau mountains bound it on the south, so that it is an intermontane basin. The climate has hot summers and chilly winters, and the surrounding mountains shield the city from the extremely cold air of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>. Before the 1960s most of the city stood on the left bank of the Varzob, but construction later carried it across.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Stone Age Mousterian tool-users lived in the Hisar Valley. The Hisar, Bishkent and Vakhsh cultures, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-tools">stone tools</a> were found within modern Dushanbe at the confluence of the Varzob and Luchob, are thought to have inhabited it in the second millennium BCE, keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, farming and weaving. The area was later settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, Greco-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hephthalites">Hephthalites</a>, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by settlements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulbuk">Hulbuk</a>, with its palace. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> was introduced in the eighth century.</p><p>The town first appears in the historical record in 1676, in a letter from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> khan Subhonquli Bahodur to Tsar Fyodor III, although the Balkh historian Mahmud ibn Wali mentioned the area in the 1630s. It was called Kasabai Dushanbe, the town of Monday, after a large bazaar held each Monday. Its place between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to Karategin and the Alay Valley, and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> through the Anzob Pass, helped the market grow. It then had a population of about 7,000 to 8,000. From the 17th to the early 20th century it was controlled at times by the Beg of Hisor, by Balkh and finally by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a>, before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> conquered it.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> captured Dushanbe in 1922, and in 1924 it became the capital of the Tajik Autonomous Soviet Socialist Republic, which began its rapid growth. When the Tajik SSR separated from the Uzbek SSR in 1929, its capital was renamed Stalinabad on 19 October 1929, incorporating the nearby districts of Shohmansur, Mavlono and Sari Osiyo. The old name returned in late 1961 as a result of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-stalinization">de-Stalinization</a>. During the middle of the 20th century the city had a majority Russian and Eastern European population. Dushanbe became the capital of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> on 9 September 1991, and the Tajik Civil War of the 1990s checked its growth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dushanbe is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> and holds most of the country's major hospitals. Its sights include the Tajikistan National Museum, the National Museum of Antiquities, Rudaki Avenue and the National Library. Its electricity is mainly hydroelectric, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurek-dam">Nurek Dam</a>, and its trolleybus system dates from 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dushanbe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A modern reader of Persian can read Ferdowsi or Hafez with far less difficulty than an English speaker reading Chaucer, although pronunciation and vocabulary have changed, and the Persian spoken in Tehran, Kabul and <b>Dushanbe</b> has developed its own features.</blockquote><a href="/language/persian">Persian · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li><li><blockquote>Many of these paintings are now in the Hermitage Museum in Saint Petersburg and the National Museum of Antiquities of Tajikistan in <b>Dushanbe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The village of <b>Dushanbe</b> was chosen as capital and grew rapidly.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajik-assr">Tajik ASSR</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital <b>Dushanbe</b> was named Stalinabad from 1929 to 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/tajik-ssr">Tajik SSR</a></li></ul></section>
